  at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m and [38;5;153mlogin[39m never even calls it. An httpOnly cookie can only be set by a server, and the browser
  handles the persisting, so the client side of your request is genuinely small; the question is whether the server side
  is inside this deliverable.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this spec commits us to building a backend, or to defining the contract a backend must satisfy and
  building the client against it. This changes the size of the work by roughly an order of magnitude, and it's cheap to
  change in one direction only — adding the server later is fine, ripping one out is waste.

  - [1mClient module + written contract + a test fake[0m — we build the identity module, [38;5;153mlogin[39m returning the server's [38;5;153muserId[39m,
    the tracking hook, and a fake that emulates the cookie so it's all testable today. The spec states exactly what the
    real backend must do ([38;5;153mSet-Cookie[39m, session lifetime, [38;5;153m/me[39m, logout). Nothing here gets thrown away when the real server
    arrives. Costs: no true end-to-end proof until it does.
  - [1mAlso build a minimal auth server here[0m — a small Node/Express service with sessions, so the whole flow really works.
    You get end-to-end verification. Costs: this repo becomes a full-stack project, and a throwaway auth server is the
    kind of thing that quietly becomes production.
  - [1mClient module only, no fake[0m — smallest diff, verification deferred entirely. Costs: nothing is testable, and the
    contract stays implicit in your head rather than written down, which is what tends to produce mismatches when the
    backend does land.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m It gets you everything you actually asked for — persistence, reuse across future forms,
  tracking — with no discarded work, and the test fake means the module isn't shipped unverified. The second is a real
  project on its own and I'd rather you choose it deliberately than inherit it from this request.
